#!/usr/bin/python3
"""oxp3-voltap - turn the ONEXPLAYER 3 volume rocker into discrete taps.

Why this exists
---------------
The OXP3 EC does not reliably deliver a key *release* for the volume rocker.
The Linux input core then keeps KEY_VOLUMEUP/DOWN logically down and the owner
of the key repeats it - kernel EV_REP soft repeat on the AT keyboard, or the
client's own "key is still down" ramp - so one press runs the mixer to 100 %
or 0 %.  Windows shows the same physical presses as single steps because its
keyboard class driver never sees a stuck make code.

Topology (decided from the devices, not from hardcoded event numbers)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
hhd owns the volume path while it runs: it grabs the physical AT keyboard
(EVIOCGRAB, one grab per device) and republishes the translated volume keys on
its own uinput device "Handheld Daemon Volume Keyboard" (phys phys-hhd-vbtn).
That virtual device is part of hhd, not a spare copy of the rocker: hhd's own
OXP keyboard producer (hhd/device/oxp/base.py, VID:PID 0001:0001, no name
filter) matches it too, so a second EVIOCGRAB on it makes hhd's grab() fail
with EBUSY.  hhd then logs "Assuming controllers disconnected", and the
emulated Steam controller (28DE:12FF) disappears for a few seconds.  That
outage happened on this unit; the rule below is therefore absolute:

  * NEVER grab a device hhd owns or opens by default.  An hhd-virtual candidate
    is a last resort that requires the explicit --allow-hhd-grab flag (and is
    never what the supported install path uses - the hhd plugin
    tools/hhd-oxp3-voltap does the normalisation inside hhd instead);
  * default order: the physical AT keyboard, but only when it is free (hhd
    stopped, controller mode disabled, or a non-hhd host);
  * when the only rocker path belongs to hhd, enter observable degraded/observe
    mode: one clear journal line, JSON status state "degraded-hhd-protected"
    and exit code 4, no grab attempt at all, retry every VOLTAP_RESCAN_S;
  * if a grab is refused, say so once (journal + JSON status + exit code) and
    keep retrying - never silently sit there.

Candidates are discovered from /proc/bus/input/devices and /sys/class/input, so
`check`, `explain`, `diagnose` and `status` work without opening /dev/input
(no root needed just to see which device *would* be used).  Explicit
VOLTAP_DEVICES / --device overrides the automatic choice.

Modes
-----
  run       daemon: grab, normalise, re-emit (default)
  check     print candidates, holders, decision, and predicted exit code
  explain   detailed per-candidate suitability explanation (alias --probe)
  probe     read-only evidence: timestamped EV_KEY table + a verdict (root)
  diagnose  capture both candidate streams for N seconds, print a grab verdict
  status    print the daemon's JSON status file
  selftest  create the uinput device and emit two taps (root)

Needs python3-evdev (already installed on Anatase, hhd depends on it).
`run` needs read access to the chosen event node and write access to
/dev/uinput: root (the system unit) is the normal way, but an input-group
member or a logind uaccess ACL (joystick-tagged virtual devices) is enough.
probe/diagnose/selftest still expect root.  On a stock host /dev/input/event*
is root:input 0660 and /dev/uinput is root:root 0600.

Author: silentsudin <25103587+silentsudin@users.noreply.github.com>
Licence: GPL-2.0-or-later - our own code, no vendor code involved.
"""

